 to be library code:"],[437,"Cost"],[460,"Coverage at a glance"],[662,"Credentials and tokens: keep them in `~/.mobsec/session/` (never in the repo, never in a screenshot)."],[512,"Current config (survives reboot):"],[641,"Daily workflow"],[360,"Daily workflow — the loop on a real engagement."],[277,"Daily workflow — the loop you will actually repeat."],[403,"Deep mode"],[577,"Defaults to exactly what the app sent (same headers, cookie, body). `-x` sends through mitmproxy (captured as evidence). `--save` writes the response body."],[381,"Deliberately excluded, with reasons:"],[9,"Design decisions worth knowing"],[557,"Device helpers"],[429,"Diff static endpoints vs captured endpoints: what the app *can* call but did not is where the interesting bugs live."],[347,"Do not trust a green `pentestctl status` alone — prove each layer with a request:"],[501,"Doing it from the host, not the UI"],[406,"Dynamic testing tells you what the app *did*; static testing tells you what it *can* do. `recon` runs the static pass in one command and produces a report you can read in two minutes."],[630,"Edited `mitm/dump.py`? `nettap off && nettap on`, otherwise you keep the old behaviour (this is how a \"fixed\" filename bug appears unfixed)."],[572,"Env: `ADDON` (dump.py), `FLOWDIR`, `INTEREST`, `PORT`. `nettap on` refuses to start if the addon or `mitmdump` is missing instead of dying silently."],[174,"Every `--patch` run should start with `--dry`. It prints the method, URL, headers and the first 300 bytes of body — enough to catch a typo before you send something destructive to a production backend."],[164,"Every interesting request on the device ends up as a file in `/tmp/mtflows` (change with `FLOWDIR`). Those files are the raw material for everything else: analysis, replay, fuzzing, evidence."],[257,"Everything here runs locally. No cloud, no extra hardware, ~20 GB of disk."],[564,"Everything is in `~/mobsec/bin` (already on `PATH`). All commands assume the emulator is running."],[587,"Expect `UNEVALUATED` on this AVD — it is Google declining to grade an emulator with no account, not a rig failure. See Play Integrity."],[399,"Exported components"],[405,"Extending it"],[574,"Finds `webview_devtools_remote_<pid>` in `/proc/net/unix` (auto-detects the foreground app when `pkg` is omitted), forwards `:9222`, prints the target list."],[339,"First real verification (5 minutes)"],[10,"Five layers, each one only there because without it a specific thing fails. The diagram is the fast way to understand the rig; the table below is the \"why\"."],[656,"Flows are `<HHMMSS>_<host>_<path>.txt` with request headers/body and response headers/body, plus a machine-readable `_index.jsonl`. Full format: Flows."],[156,"For every finding, keep the evidence triple: **the flow file** (`/tmp/mtflows/…txt`), **the replay command** that reproduces it, and **the app state** (which `snap` image, which user, which Frida profile). `_index.jsonl` plus a one-line `replay` command is the cheapest possible repro packet."],[212,"For hybrid apps this is the highest-value tool in the rig: no hooks, full DOM, console, network view and storage."],[592,"For permanent LAN sharing there is a user unit, `mobsec-docs.service`\n(`systemctl --user enable --now mobsec-docs`), and `sudo ufw allow 8000/tcp` once — `ufw`'s default\ninbound policy is `DROP`, so a bound port is not a reachable port."],[158,"Format"],[542,"Frida"],[182,"Frida & instrumentation"],[191,"Frida is not \"installed\" here, it is **wired**: the agent autostarts as a Magisk module under a different name, listens on loopback only, and the host side is a set of script profiles that answer specific questions."],[211,"Frida's REPL needs a tty. `frida -U -f pkg < /dev/null` exits immediately, and `pkill -f 'frida -U -f pkg'` can kill the shell that issued it. Use a fifo:"],[77,"From the target app's behaviour, in order of strictness:"],[221,"Glossary"],[291,"Google receives the request and declines to grade it: the device is an emulator and **no Google account is signed in**. Both conditions must be satisfied for a verdict to exist at all — and even with an account, a Play Store emulator image still cannot return `MEETS_DEVICE_INTEGRITY`."],[538,"Health & control"],[532,"Hiding does not disturb spoofing: after HMA was enabled, KeyAttestation still reported the keybox chain (`CN=Droid CA2, O=Google LLC`) and `Bootloader is locked`."],[531,"HMA never hides a target app from itself, so the detector still lists `icu.nullptr.applistdetector` — that is correct behaviour, not a leak."],[518,"HMA-OSS (`hma_oss_zygisk`, manager `org.frknkrc44.hma_oss`) hooks package queries inside a chosen app and filters the answer. It is **scoped per target**: you say \"when *this* app asks, act as if these packages do not exist\"."],[499,"HMA-OSS: hiding packages from one app at a time"],[562,"Host environment"],[459,"Host tools"],[68,"How it behaves:"],[304,"HTTPS interception"],[611,"If `pentestctl status` is green but the app is silent, the problem is almost always **inside the app** (dead proxy in a cached connection, app not restarted, wrong package)."],[515,"If an app is in the denylist, Magisk strips module mounts from it — which is exactly where TrickyStore and PIF need to be. Hiding by denylisting is incompatible with spoofing. Choose one per app."],[492,"If any of those drift, run `pentestctl fix` and read Troubleshooting."],[274,"If something is red, `pentestctl fix` repairs the two things that break on every reboot (`adb reverse` and the mitm addon), then see Troubleshooting."],[633,"If step 5 is stale, reboot once — Cert-Fixer only mounts at `post-fs-data`."],[640,"If the demo app also fails, the rig is broken. If only your target fails, the target has an additional defence — collect `fspawn … trace` output and read the `rootcheck`/`tls` lines before guessing."],[650,"If you changed anything device-wide (new module, new CA, new keybox), `snap save` a **new** snapshot name instead of overwriting `logged-in` — snapshots carry the mount state, and a stale snapshot can bring back a broken CA mount."],[523,"If you configure HMA in its UI and press Back, nothing is written — `AppSettingsV2Fragment` persists in `onPause`. Press HOME (or leave the app) to commit. This is why hand-editing `config.json` with `hma` is the less surprising path."],[297,"In order of cost:"],[84,"Install **KeyAttestation** (`io.github.vvb2060.keyattestation`, in `apks/`) and generate a key:"],[317,"Installed at `/data/misc/user/0/cacerts-added/c8750f0d.0`; Cert-Fixer copies and bind-mounts it into `/apex/com.android.conscrypt/cacerts` for every process, and does the same inside PID 1's mount namespace so late-started system services inherit it."],[361,"Interception — why pinning bypass works, and what still fails."],[63,"It is a **hardware/RKP** chain — the server can tell software chains apart, and they are rejected by anything modern."],[359,"It is a plain MkDocs site: no auth, no TLS. Anyone on that network can read the whole rig, including\nwhich target you are testing — put it behind Tailscale or an SSH tunnel\n(`ssh -L 8000:127.0.0.1:8000 host`) if the network is not yours."],[292,"It removes the `UNEVALUATED` state and replaces it with a clean `MEETS_BASIC_INTEGRITY` / no `DEVICE` result. It does not unlock anything, and it puts a real account on a rooted emulator."],[216,"It replaces `getSharedPreferences()` globally and Chromium then dies with\n`NullPointerException: … SharedPreferences$Editor.apply() on a null object reference`.\nKeep it out of profiles for any app with a WebView; load it by hand only for native-only apps."],[74,"It runs automatically through a **systemd user timer** (`mobsec-keybox.timer`: 10 min after boot, then daily with a randomised delay, `Persistent=true`):"],[13,"It runs with KVM acceleration (`/dev/kvm`, user in `kvm` group), 4 vCPUs, and GPU on. Boot from cold is ~90 s; from a snapshot, ~25 s."],[629,"Its webroot has megabytes of i18n JSON and floods your terminal (and your context). Read specific files."],[449,"Keep at most a handful: each is ~5 GB."],[45,"Keeping it alive: `keybox-refresh`"],[43,"Keybox lifecycle"],[67,"Keyboxes die quietly — the leaf expires and the server starts answering `4202` again with no other symptom. `keybox-refresh` is the boring fix:"],[461,"Known-good invariants"],[428,"Launch with `fspawn com.example.app web` (or `intercept`) and capture the real request set."],[79,"Leaf public key matches the challenge-signed data."],[101,"Legend: **✔** a command does it · **~** partial, read the note · **✘** impossible or pointless here."],[456,"Live status"],[608,"Logs worth knowing"],[661,"Long captures: `nettap status` prints flow count and the addon path, so you can tell \"no traffic\" from \"addon not loaded\"."],[15,"Magisk 31.1 specifics that bite:"],[497,"Magisk denylist: keep it empty"],[346,"Magisk's \"install → patch image → additional setup → reboot\" flow is UI-driven. `rootAVD.sh` pauses for it; the `GUIDE-from-scratch.md` section 4 walks the exact taps, and `uitap` can drive most of them by text."],[17,"Magisk's denylist is **empty** by design here. Adding an app would strip our modules (TrickyStore, PIF) from exactly the app that needs them."],[289,"Measured on this AVD, twice, before and after PlayIntegrityFix:"],[527,"Measured on this rig, detector scoped, before vs after reboot:"],[280,"Measuring it on this rig"],[215,"Medusa's modules assume the framework's helpers (`colorLog`, `Module.findExportByName`). The exporter injects a shim: `Color`, `colorLog`, `Options` defaults, `__mod('libc.so', …)`, plus Frida-17 polyfills for `Module.findExportByName` / `getExportByName`."],[576,"Merges `document.cookie`, `localStorage`, `sessionStorage` from the WebView with the newest flow's cookie header (httpOnly cookies are invisible to JS). `TARGET=target session` restricts the page match."],[575,"Minimal CDP client (python3 + `websockets`). `eval` awaits promises; `watch` streams console/network events."],[250,"mobsec — an Android pentest rig that actually works"],[53,"Module id `tricky_store`, manager UI in `com.example.target`-style apps is not needed — it is driven by two files."],[458,"Modules"],[628,"Multi-line snippets with `for`/`while` fail with `syntax error: unexpected 'do'`. Use `asu '…'`, which writes a script and runs it."],[450,"Name by intent (`logged-in`, `pre-fuzz`, `clean-no-modules`), not by date."],[170,"Names carry no date (only `HHMMSS`), so **never sort flows by name across days** — `session`, `replay` and `snap` sort by mtime."],[81,"No serial is revoked, nothing expired."],[624,"No tty → REPL dies. Use a fifo:"],[427,"Note the **exported** components and any endpoint that looks internal (`/admin`, `/internal`, versioned staging hosts)."],[166,"Notes:"],[61,"Nothing in the chain is **expired**."],[153,"OEM-specific bugs (the emulator is AOSP + Google image, no vendor blobs)."],[51,"On an emulator that chain says *\"Droid Unregistered Device CA, Google Test LLC\"* — an obvious emulator. On a real phone it says *\"hardware-backed, verified boot green, your device\"*. Even with a software keybox the chain is signed by an **expired** AOSP intermediate. Both get rejected."],[631,"On x86_64 it silently never loads (`zygiskd status` stops listing `playintegrityfix`). Use the 4.9 x86_64 build."],[165,"One file per flow, named `<HHMMSS>_<host>_<path>.txt`:"],[519,"One JSON document, schema `configVersion: 95`, with a `scope` object keyed by target package. Each entry is an `AppConfig`:"],[380,"Only Magisk's first-run flow: *Install → Patch firmware/image → Select from storage → fakeboot.img → OK → additional setup → reboot*, plus granting `su` to shell. `GUIDE §4` has the exact taps; `uitap` can drive them by text, but expect to babysit it once."],[430,"Only then start `replay` fuzzing."],[345,"Order matters: `install-device.sh` reboots the device, `setup-net.sh` must run **after** the first boot with modules installed, and `setup-keybox.sh` last (it validates against the live device's TrickyStore path)."],[610,"Ordered by how often each one actually happens."],[334,"Output `<HHMMSS>_<host>_<path>.txt`, plus `_index.log` (human) and `_index.jsonl` (machine)."],[407,"Output:"],[400,"Packers / anti-analysis (APKiD)"],[173,"Patch syntax is a small JSONPath: `a.b`, `items[0].id`, `a.b[2].c`. Values are parsed as JSON when possible (`42`, `true`, `\"str\"`), else kept as strings."],[337,"Path A — the device already exists"],[338,"Path B — rebuild from zero"],[180,"Patterns worth grepping for in a fresh corpus:"],[90,"Pentest checklist (mapped to this rig)"],[154,"Performance/timing side channels that depend on real CPU and memory pressure."],[307,"Pinning"],[457,"Platform"],[279,"Play Integrity (the other attestation)"],[49,"Play Integrity is a different problem"],[287,"Play Integrity is Google's own server-side verdict. You cannot compute it locally, and the request only works for a Play-distributed, Play-signed app with a Play Console project — which is why pentest tooling ships its own checker APK."],[586,"Play Integrity verdict from SPIC, scraped out of logcat."],[151,"Play Integrity verdicts (why)."],[88,"Play Integrity's `MEETS_DEVICE_INTEGRITY` cannot be faked on an emulator; measured here as **UNEVALUATED** (Google declines to grade an emulator with no account) before and after PlayIntegrityFix. This rig does not solve PI, and does not need to for apps that use raw key attestation. The mechanics, the `integrity` tool and the escape routes are on the Play Integrity page."],[323,"Plus two scripts that make interception work without a system proxy:"],[520,"Preset names (they are the module's, not yours): apps = `accessibility_apps, custom_rom, detector_apps, root_apps, shizuku_dhizuku, sus_apps, xposed`; settings = `accessibility, dev_options, input_method`. The five app presets above are what you want against a root-detector: Magisk, KeyAttestation, Frida tooling, other test apps, Xposed-era leftovers."],[185,"Profiles"],[573,"Profiles: `trace` (default), `web`, `attest`, `spy`, `intercept`, `full`, `raw`. See Frida."],[326,"Proof, reproducible on this device:"],[171,"Quick analysis:"],[336,"Quickstart"],[276,"Quickstart — a device that is already built, or build it from zero."],[421,"Random 30–40 char strings tagged `Artifactory_Password` are frequently obfuscation noise; grep the decompiled tree for the value before writing a finding."],[409,"Read this with suspicion. APKiD matches library code too:"],[56,"Reads `tee_status`; on an emulator `teeBroken=true` is expected."],[69,"Reads the device keybox, runs `kbcheck.py --summary` (`device=… leaf_not_after=… sig=… revoked=…`)."],[423,"Real finds look like: hardcoded bearer tokens, private endpoints, staging hosts, cloud storage buckets."],[363,"Rebuild from scratch"],[607,"Recovery runbook"],[283,"Related traps"],[196,"Renaming matters: the string `frida-server` and the default port `27042` are what most detectors grep for in `/proc/net/tcp`, process lists and file paths. `hluda` is what a heavily-used public build calls itself; on loopback it is also invisible to the LAN."],[160,"Replay"],[99,"Reporting habit"],[582,"Reports the biggest files, every SQLite database with tables/row counts, `shared_prefs` and JSON lines that look like secrets, and pulls `/sdcard/Android/data/<pkg>` too. Writes `summary.txt` next to the extracted tree."],[335,"Restart after editing: `nettap off && nettap on`. mitmproxy does **not** hot-reload addons."],[493,"Root hiding"],[504,"Root is required for everything else here, so hiding it is a permanent tax. This rig hides the *mechanisms* (su binary, Magisk daemon, Zygisk libs, Frida) rather than trying to make a detector believe the device is unmodified — the attestation layer handles the \"is this a real phone\" question separately."],[584,"Root-hiding scope editor for HMA-OSS (writes `config.json` over `su`, reloads live)."],[424,"Rule-based Android checks (exported components, weak crypto, WebView settings, backup flags…). Fast, noisy, useful as a checklist. Zero findings is a valid result — not proof of safety."],[652,"Rules of thumb:"],[447,"Rules:"],[588,"Runs a root shell snippet without the `adb shell su -c \"…\"` quoting nightmare:"],[310,"Sanity checks that catch most \"interception is broken\" reports"],[402,"SAST (mobsfscan)"],[401,"Secrets / endpoints (apkleaks)"],[565,"Sections and what they read:"],[648,"Session hygiene"],[367,"Sharing the rig with a colleague"],[340,"Sharing this site"],[462,"Snapshot taken **2026-11-24 20:10 IST** on the working rig. `pentestctl status` is the live truth — this page records what \"green\" looked like so you can tell drift from breakage."],[433,"Snapshots"],[446,"Snapshots capture **mount state**, so a snapshot taken while a module was broken keeps restoring the broken mount. Symptoms: Cert-Fixer log says it ran, but GMS still cannot TLS. Fix: cold boot (`snap boot`), verify, then `snap save` again under a new name."],[418,"So: no app-level emulator check in the DEX. The `DexGuard` tag was R8 single-letter-package confusion. Ten minutes of reading beat a day of chasing a phantom."],[82,"Sometimes: `verifiedBootState` = green, `deviceLocked` = true, `osVersion` sane."],[66,"Source: `redteam-git/RED-TEAM-KEYBOX` publishes a base64 blob at `…/main/conf` (refreshed every 4 h upstream). `setup-keybox.sh --file <xml>` installs a local file; `--check` validates only."],[163,"Spotting the interesting things"],[255,"Start reading here"],[396,"Static recon"],[70,"Stays put unless the keybox is close to expiry or already dead."],[189,"Stolen modules (Medusa)"],[179,"Storage layout:"],[595,"Symptom → cause"],[589,"Text-driven UI automation (`uiautomator dump` under the hood) — survives layout changes that break fixed coordinates:"],[445,"That is why the documented restore sequence is:"],[319,"That module only mounts in `service.sh`, i.e. **after** GMS and Play Store have already started, so they kept their original trust store and failed with `Client TLS handshake failed … does not trust the proxy's certificate for play-fe.googleapis.com`. Cert-Fixer hooks `post-fs-data` instead. It is installed, `trustusercerts` is disabled via `touch /data/adb/modules/trustusercerts/disable`."],[57,"The `DeviceID` attribute is just a label the upstream keybox author typed. For the public\n`RED-TEAM-KEYBOX` source it is literally a Telegram advert\n(`DeviceID=\"RKP keybox. Join my group: t.me/redteamchannell\"`). TrickyStore never uses it and the\nserver validates the **chain**, not the label — so it is normal for it to look like spam and to\nchange on every `keybox-refresh`. `pentestctl status` prints it tagged *(cosmetic upstream label)*."],[524,"The `FileObserver` is constructed at boot even when the file is missing. If HMA has never written a config on this boot, your first `hma hide` needs one reboot before it has any effect. Check with `hma show` — if it is empty and you just created a scope, reboot, then re-apply."],[533,"The 10-mount difference is the honest answer to \"is injection scoped\": non-target apps see zero module mounts."],[309,"The addon"],[408,"The attack surface that does not require the app to be running. For each component: `permission`, `actions`, `categories`, `data` (scheme/host/path/mime). Anything exported without a signature-level permission is worth a `adb shell am start` / `am broadcast` probe."],[258,"The attestation layer makes this device claim to be a different physical phone, and the keybox in use is not ours. Use it against apps you are permitted to test. Nothing in this rig is anonymous: traffic goes out from your own IP."],[306,"The CA, exactly"],[305,"The chain"],[75,"The current leaf expires **2026-12-04**; `pentestctl status` prints the remaining days on every run, so the timer is a safety net and not the primary signal."],[183,"The device side"],[591,"The documentation site you are reading, served locally."],[275,"The emulator boots a **Magisk-patched ramdisk**, so `su` works from the first second. Magisk loads three Zygisk modules that matter: **TrickyStore** fakes the hardware keystore's attestation chain for packages listed in `target.txt`, **PlayIntegrityFix** tries (and fails, on an emulator) to satisfy Play Integrity, and **Zygisk Next** does the injecting in anonymous memory. **Cert-Fixer** bind-mounts our mitmproxy CA into every process's trust store, including system ones. **frida-server** runs renamed as `hluda` under a small custom module, listening on loopback only. On the host, `nettap` keeps mitmproxy and the `adb reverse` tunnel alive, `fspawn` starts an app under Frida with a chosen script profile, and `replay` re-issues anything you captured."],[500,"The file that matters"],[594,"The five-minute triage"],[344,"The full beginner walkthrough (SDK install, AVD tuning, rootAVD/Magisk UI clicks, module URLs, CA install, verification) lives in `GUIDE-from-scratch.md` in the repo root (open it directly, it is not part of this site). Short version:"],[311,"The goal is boring reliability: every app on the device, including Play Store and GMS, decrypts through mitmproxy, and apps that pin certificates still connect."],[167,"The headers block is mitmproxy's `Headers` repr on **one line** — parse with `ast.literal_eval` after stripping the `Headers` prefix (that is what `replay` does)."],[581,"The honest replacement for `adb backup` (blocked on API 31+): root-tars the app's private dir, pulls it, and summarises."],[184,"The host side"],[159,"The index"],[2,"The layers, in dependency order"],[186,"The local script set"],[649,"The loop that matters: **restore → verify → instrument → capture → replay → document.**"],[281,"The modules, and why they do not help here"],[44,"The one that works right now"],[253,"The one-paragraph mental model"],[387,"The recipient still needs **their own** `adb` device and must run `setup-net.sh` so their mitmproxy CA (not ours) is installed. `SHARING.md` in the archive carries the exact run order and this table."],[62,"The serial numbers are **not on Google's revocation list** (`https://android.googleapis.com/attestation/status`)."],[580,"The sheet lists exported activities/services/receivers with their required permissions, content providers (including `exported=false` + `grantUriPermissions=true`), every `scheme://host` deep link, and `pm get-app-links` state."],[354,"The site is served by MkDocs on the host. By default it listens on loopback only:"],[303,"The verdict is a multi-line pretty-printed document in logcat. Closing braces of nested objects are indented; only the final one is flush left. Any scraper that stops at the first `}` truncates the JSON and reports \"no verdict\" — a bug that cost an hour here."],[76,"The well-known Yurikey keybox had a perfect hardware chain — and its leaf serial `bb0d83fa2a47a1ae54c8c359778aaf35` was on Google's revocation list (2222 entries). Everything looked right in the UI, and the server still said no. Always check revocation, not just expiry."],[496,"The whitelist trap"],[658,"Then write findings with the evidence pointer (`flow file`, `timestamp`, `endpoint`, `repro command`). A finding without a repro command is a rumour."],[517,"There is no stub-repackaging option in Settings and no CLI equivalent. Package-level hiding is HMA-OSS's job; keep that expectation straight when a detector lists installed packages."],[100,"This is the HackTricks/OWASP-MASTG Android checklist, rewritten as *\"which command in this rig does that, and where does it stop working\"*. Use it so you do not reinvent steps — and so you do not claim coverage the emulator cannot give you."],[50,"This is the part that usually makes people give up on emulator-based testing. A modern app asks the **Android keystore** to generate a key with a server-chosen challenge, and the keystore returns a certificate chain signed by Google's servers describing the device. The app ships that chain to its backend, which decides whether to let you in."],[358,"To keep it up permanently (survives logout/reboot, restarts on failure):"],[162,"Token harvesting"],[548,"Traffic"],[596,"Trap list (all hit for real here)"],[498,"Traps"],[42,"TrickyStore"],[83,"TrickyStore supplies 1–5 for keystore users, but the **revocation list** is checked server-side and cannot be spoofed locally. That is why a revoked keybox is worthless even though the UI shows a beautiful chain."],[71,"Tries sources in order — `redteam`, then `yuri` — accepting only payloads that `kbcheck.py --strict` accepts (signatures, chain, expiry, of the revocation list)."],[593,"Troubleshooting"],[362,"Troubleshooting — the eight traps, in the order they usually hit."],[579,"Turns the manifest into copy-paste attack commands — one `adb shell am start|broadcast|content` line per exported component and deep link."],[370,"Two artefacts describe a rebuild:"],[27,"Two details make this painless:"],[284,"Two different things get called \"attestation\" in Android pentests, and confusing them wastes days:"],[169,"Two hosts with the same path no longer collide: the host is part of the name, and a `-2`/`-3` suffix is added if the same second produces the same host+path."],[320,"Two independent mechanisms, both from HTTP Toolkit's Frida scripts (`frida/httptoolkit/`), loaded by `fspawn <pkg> intercept`:"],[342,"Two paths: the device already exists (this laptop), or you are rebuilding it on a fresh machine."],[355,"Two things have to be true for another device to load it:"],[516,"Two-argument form kills `magiskd`; afterwards `magisk -v` says\n`Cannot connect to daemon: Connection refused (os error 111)` and `su` is gone with it. Only a reboot recovers. Always pass a single package argument."],[585,"Un-hiding needs a reboot (uid cache); a `config.json` created *after* boot needs one reboot before it is watched. See Root hiding."],[439,"Under the hood:"],[397,"Usage"],[526,"Use `apks/ApplistDetector.V2.4.apk` (`icu.nullptr.applistdetector`, Dr-TSNG) as the detector:"],[432,"Useful manual follow-ups it does not do:"],[368,"Verification after a rebuild"],[514,"Verified and reverted. Keep `default`, and keep the app you are testing **out** of Magisk's denylist."],[318,"Verify for a specific process:"],[502,"Verifying hiding actually works"],[48,"Verifying on-device"],[369,"Version pinning that matters"],[422,"Version strings (`1.0.0.2`) match `IP_Address`; ignore."],[188,"WebView = CDP"],[8,"What fails if you remove a layer"],[494,"What is hidden, and how"],[366,"What is interactive"],[308,"What still resists"],[435,"What survives a restore"],[398,"What the report contains, and how to read it"],[47,"What the server actually validates"],[503,"What this does not hide"],[251,"What you get"],[436,"When a snapshot is a trap"],[190,"When Frida itself is the problem"],[609,"When nothing makes sense"],[395,"When you bump a version, re-run the verification list top to bottom before trusting the rig."],[282,"When your target actually gates on Play Integrity"],[254,"Where things live"],[341,"Where to go next"],[155,"Whether a leaked key matters — that is a server-side validation question, and the answer changes per target."],[365,"Why the order is fixed"],[161,"Why this beats Burp here"],[327,"Without the profile the same button says **Failed** and no flow appears."],[590,"Wrapper around the pinned `apktool_3.0.3.jar` (`apktool d app.apk -o out`)."],[578,"Writes `~/.mobsec/recon/<pkg>-<ts>/report.md` + `raw/`. See Static recon."],[495,"Zygisk Next knobs"],[25,"Zygisk Next's memory type is set to **anonymous** so module libs never appear in `/proc/<pid>/maps` as files."]],"type":"string"},"page_id":{"docs":{"1":29,"2":30,"3":31,"4":32,"5":33,"6":34,"7":35,"8":36,"9":37,"10":38,"11":39,"12":40,"13":41,"14":42,"15":43,"16":44,"17":45,"18":46,"19":47,"20":48,"21":49,"22":50,"23":51,"24":52,"25":53,"26":54,"27":55,"28":56,"29":57,"30":58,"31":59,"32":60,"33":61,"34":62,"35":63,"36":64,"37":65,"38":66,"39":67,"40":68,"41":69,"4